== Chapter IV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The last Principle; of the usefulness of Mortification. The Vigor and Comfort of our spiritual Life depend on our Mortification. In what sense. Not absolutely and necessarily.] Ps 88. Heman'#emph[s Condition. Not as on the next and immediate Cause. As a Means: by removing of the Contrary. The desperate Effects of unmortified Lust: it weakens the Soul]\; Ps 38:3, 8. #emph[sundry ways: and darkens it. All Graces improved by the Mortification of Sin. The best Evidence of Sincerity.]]
]

#smallcaps[THE] last Principle I shall insist on, omitting, 1. The #emph[Necessity] of #emph[Mortification] unto Life: And, 2. The #emph[Certainty] of Life upon #emph[Mortification]\; is,

#emph[That the Life, Vigor, and Comfort of our spiritual Life depends much on our Mortification of Sin.]

Strength, and Comfort, and Power, and Peace in our walking with God, are the things of our desires. Were any of us asked seriously, What it is that troubles us, we must #emph[refer] it to one of these heads; either we want #emph[strength,] or #emph[power, vigor] and #emph[life,] in our #emph[Obedience,] in our #emph[walking with God]\; or we want #emph[peace, comfort,] and #emph[consolation] therein. Whatever it is, that may befall a #emph[Believer,] that doth not belong to one of these #emph[two heads,] doth not deserve to be mentioned in the #emph[days] of our Complaints. Now all these do much depend on a #emph[constant] Course of #emph[Mortification]\; concerning which observe,

1\. I do not say they #emph[proceed] from it; as though they were #emph[necessarily] tied to it. A man may be carried on in a #emph[constant] course of #emph[Mortification] all his #emph[days,] and yet perhaps never enjoy good day of Peace and Consolation. So it was with #emph[Heman, Psal.] 88. His life was a life of #emph[perpetual Mortification,] and walking with God, yet #emph[terrors] and #emph[wounds] were his portion all his days: But God singled out #emph[Heman] (a choice Friend) to make him an Example to them that afterwards should be in distress. Canst thou complain if it be no otherwise with thee than it was with #emph[Heman,] that eminent servant of God? And this shall be his praise to the end of the World; God makes it his Prerogative to speak Peace and Consolation: Isa 57:18, 19. I will do that work, says God; I will #emph[comfort] him, v. 18. But how? By an immediate work of the #emph[new Creation,] I #emph[Create it,] says, God. The use of #emph[means] for the obtaining of #emph[peace] is #emph[ours]\; the #emph[bestowing] of it is God's #emph[Prerogative.]

2\. In the ways instituted by God for to give us #emph[Life, Vigor, Courage] and #emph[Consolation, Mortification] is not one of the immediate Causes of it. They are the #emph[Privileges] of our #emph[Adoption,] made known to our Souls, that give us #emph[immediately] these things. #emph[The Spirit bearing witness with our spirits that we are the Children of God:] giving us a #emph[new Name,] and a #emph[white Stone]\; Adoption and Justification, that is, as to the sense and knowledge of them, are the immediate Causes (in the hand of the Spirit) of these things. But this I say,

3\. In our #emph[ordinary walking] with God, and in an #emph[ordinary] Course of his #emph[dealing with us,] the #emph[vigor] and #emph[comfort] of our spiritual Lives depends much on our #emph[Mortification,] not only as a #emph[Causa sine qua non,] but as a thing that hath an effectual #emph[influence] thereinto. For,

==== As a Means, by Removing the Contrary

First, This alone keeps Sin from #emph[depriving] us of the #emph[one] and the #emph[other]: Every #emph[unmortified] sin will certainly do #emph[two things]:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It will #emph[weaken] the Soul, and deprive it of its #emph[vigor.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. It will #emph[darken] the Soul, and deprive it of its Comfort and Peace.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1.) It #emph[weakens] the Soul, and deprives it of its Strength: when #emph[David] had for a while #emph[harbored] an unmortified Lust in his heart, it broke all his #emph[Bones,] and left him no spiritual #emph[strength]\; hence he complained that he was #emph[sick, weak, wounded, faint]\; there is (saith he) no soundness in me, Ps 38:3. #emph[I am feeble and sore broken,] v. 8. Yea I cannot #emph[so much as look] up, Ps 40:12. An unmortified Lust will drink up the Spirit, and all the vigor of the Soul, and weaken it for all Duties. For,]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It #emph[untunes] and unframes the Heart itself, by entangling its Affections. It diverts the #emph[heart] from that spiritual Frame that is required for #emph[vigorous] Communion with God. It lays hold on the Affections, rendering its Object beloved and desirable; so expelling the #emph[love of the Father,] 1 John 2:15. Chapter 3:17. So that the Soul cannot say #emph[uprightly] and truly to God, Thou art my #emph[portion,] having something else that it loves. Fear, Desire, Hope, which are the choice Affections of the Soul, that should be full of God, will be one way or other entangled with it.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. It fills the #emph[Thoughts] with #emph[Contrivances] about it. Thoughts are the great #emph[Purveyors] of the Soul, to bring in #emph[provision] to satisfy its Affections; and if Sin remain #emph[unmortified] in the Heart, they must ever and anon be making provision for the #emph[flesh,] to fulfill the Lusts thereof. They must #emph[glaze, adorn,] and #emph[dress] the #emph[Objects] of the Flesh, and bring them home to give satisfaction. And this they are able to do, in the service of a defiled Imagination, beyond all expression.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. It #emph[breaks] out and actually #emph[hinders] Duty. The #emph[ambitious] man must be #emph[studying,] and the #emph[worldling] must be working or contriving, and the #emph[sensual] vain person providing himself for #emph[vanity,] when they should be engaged in the Worship of God.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Were this my present business, to set forth the Breaches, Ruin, Weakness, Desolations, that one #emph[unmortified Lust] will bring upon a Soul, this Discourse must be extended much beyond my Intendment.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2) As Sin #emph[weakens,] so it #emph[darkens] the Soul. It is a #emph[cloud,] a #emph[thick cloud,] that spreads itself over the Face of the Soul, and intercepts all the #emph[beams] of Gods Love and Favor. It takes away all sense of the Privilege of our Adoption: and if the Soul begins to gather up Thoughts of #emph[Consolation,,] Sin quickly scatters them. Of which afterwards.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now in this regard doth the #emph[vigor] and #emph[power] of our #emph[spiritual life] depend on our #emph[Mortification.] It is the only Means of the Removal of that, which will allow us neither the #emph[one] nor the #emph[other.] Men that are #emph[sick] and #emph[wounded] under the power of #emph[Lust,] make many Applications for Help; they cry to God, when the Perplexity of their Thoughts overwhelms them; #emph[even to God] do they cry, but are not delivered; in vain do they use #emph[many Remedies, they shall not be healed.] So Hos 5:13. #emph[Ephraim saw his sickness, and Judah his wound,] and attempted sundry remedies, nothing will do, until they come (v. 15) to #emph[acknowledge their Offense.] Men may see their Sickness and Wounds, but yet if they make not due Applications their Cure will not be effected.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, Mortification prunes all the #emph[Graces] of God, and makes #emph[room] for them in our hearts, to grow. The Life and Vigor of our #emph[spiritual Lives] consists in the vigor and flourishing of the Plants of Grace in our Hearts. Now as you may see in a #emph[Garden,] let there be a precious #emph[herb] planted, and let the Ground be untilled, and #emph[weeds] grow about it, perhaps it will #emph[live] still, but be a poor #emph[withering, unuseful] thing; you must #emph[look] and #emph[search] for it, and sometimes can scarce find it; and when you do, you can scarce know it, whether it be the #emph[Plant] you look for or no; and suppose it be; you can make no use of it at all: when let another of the same kind be set in #emph[ground,] naturally as #emph[barren] and #emph[bad] as the other; but let it be well #emph[weeded,] and every thing that is #emph[noxious] and #emph[hurtful] removed from it, it #emph[flourishes] and #emph[thrives]\; you may see it at first #emph[look] into the Garden, and have it for your use when you please. So it is with the #emph[Graces] of the Spirit that are planted in our hearts: That is true; they #emph[are] still, they abide in a heart where there is some Neglect of Mortification, but they are ready to die; Rev 3:2 they are withering and decaying. The heart is like the #emph[Sluggards Field,] so over-grown with #emph[weeds,] that you can scarce see the good Corn. Such a man may search for Faith, Love and Zeal, and scarce be able to find any; and if he do discover that these Graces are #emph[there,] yet alive, and sincere; yet they are so weak, so clogged with Lusts, that they are of very little use; they remain indeed, but are ready to die. But now let the Heart be cleansed by #emph[Mortification,] the Weeds of Lust constantly and daily rooted up, (as they spring daily, #emph[Nature] being their proper soil,) let Room be made for Grace to thrive and flourish; how will every Grace act its part, and be ready for every use and purpose!]

Thirdly, As to our #emph[Peace]\; as there is nothing that hath any Evidence of #emph[Sincerity] without it, so I know nothing that hath such an Evidence of Sincerity in it; which is no small Foundation of our Peace: Mortification is the #emph[Souls] vigorous Opposition to #emph[self]\; wherein Sincerity is most evident.
